## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small, realistic three-valued constraint solver for policy-like rules, relevant to data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic (T, F, U)
- Implication as ¬p ∨ q
- Backtracking with domain pruning (unit-like propagation)
- Constraint propagation in CSPs with partial information

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued logic helpers
const T = 'T', F = 'F', U = 'U';
function not(v){ if(v===T) return F; if(v===F) return T; return U; }
function and(a,b){ if(a===F||b===F) return F; if(a===U||b===U) return U; return T; }
function or(a,b){ if(a===T||b===T) return T; if(a===U||b===U) return U; return F; }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// Placeholder: propagate constraints on a set of variables using these helpers
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to N checks with arbitrary Horn-like constraints and report unsat cores under partial information?
- What data structures (domains, watch lists) would you use to implement efficient propagation and backtracking?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued logic, especially how disjunction, conjunction, and implication interact under uncertainty. It also probes backtracking strategies in a non-classical setting.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued semantics and default propagation
- Backtracking with partial assignments
- Constraint propagation under non-monotonic connectives

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued helpers (short version)
const vals = ['T','F','U'];
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function or(a,b){ return (a==='T' || b==='T')?'T': (a==='F' && b==='F')?'F':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return (a==='F' || b==='F')?'F': (a==='T' && b==='T')?'T':'U'; }
function imp(a,b){ // a→b := ¬a ∨ b
  return or(not(a), b);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to translate graph-based access policy constraints into a three-valued logical solver while managing constraint satisfaction risks. The directed graph structure introduces transitive reasoning requirements, making the problem more realistic than handling independent logical clauses.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic propagation using Kleene semantics (T > U > F)
- Arc-consistency enforcement on directed constraint graphs
- Not-both-true constraint handling and conflict detection
- Backtracking strategies for partial truth assignments

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vals, edges) {
  // vals: {N1:'T'|'F'|'U',...}
  // edges: [[from, to], ...]
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'F') {
        return false; // Constraint violated
      }
      if (vals[from] === 'T' && vals[to] === 'U') {
        vals[to] = 'T';
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests reasoning with temporal and tri-valued logic, combining forward propagation with unknowns and discrete time.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics
- Temporal horizons and propagation
- Backtracking with partial assignments

## Code Example
```javascript
function check(trace){
  for(let t=0;t<3;t++){
    if(trace.X1[t]==="T" && trace.X2[t+1]==="F") return false;
    if(trace.X2[t]==="F" && trace.X3[t+1]!=="T") return false;
    if(trace.X3[t]==="U" && trace.X1[t+2]!=="U") return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

